import{_ as i,P as r,d as a,e as n,x as o,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.5 HV05 Action Uptake Layer (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.5 HV05 Action Uptake Layer (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1566,"xkLessons":{"A.5":{"title":"A.5 HV05 Action-uptake layer (complete interface card)","lead":"HV05 distinguishes uptake, costs, benefits, authority to stop, and six types of responsibility. Its five routes separately address current functions, historical traces, and two intertemporal explanations; none creates an unlimited obligation to bear burdens.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3918–3983; original-site location /theory/appendix-a/a-05#section-a-5. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical Chinese research manuscript and slices remain downloadable in their original versions for checking.","Short quotation: ‘Capacity for uptake does not become an obligation, and CV does not become RS’ (translated from Chinese). CV is a carrier that actually executes, transmits, maintains, records, cares, or repairs, and may be a person, post, procedure, facility, or institution; RS is a party bearing the relevant type of responsibility. Who acts, who incurs costs, who benefits, who has authority to stop, and who bears which responsibility must be kept separate. A facility's ability to transmit data does not make it a bearer of moral or legal responsibility. [T-main；T-H2]","HV05-R0-carrier-responsibility-split registers CV, cost bearers of the same type, beneficiaries, authority to stop, RS, resources, and capacity under H2, stopping at current classification and gaps. H2 is an operational classification rule, not an empirical causal law. R0 can identify mismatches between tasks and resources, but does not automatically make the most visible executor the principal party responsible for decisions, authorization, benefits, or remedies. [T-main；T-H2]","HV05-R1-functional-carrier-effect requires a qualifying G2 to support the channel effect of specified carrier replacement, interruption, replenishment, or load reduction on a preselected functional outcome, plus CAUSAL/E4. It supports only measured dimensions of function, capacity, delay, or loss. Fewer labor hours for one task does not establish lower overall burdens; improved function does not create an obligation for continued sacrifice or permission to reallocate resources. [T-main；T-G2]","HV05-R2-intertemporal-reproduction requires G2 support for the current channel, plus G3 establishing that historical variables provide a conditional increment for subsequent uptake/reproduction beyond the current representation, again with CAUSAL/E4. This historical increment does not mean merely ‘it happened long ago’ or ‘the old rule remains.’ It means that, with the current representation and other conditions fixed, a historical variable still provides a proportionate increment for the specified subsequent outcome. This does not imply historical fate, irreversibility, or attribution of responsibility. [T-main；T-G3]","HV05-R3-historical-carrier-trace is the H5 trace route. It requires supported status for the uniquely preselected concrete carrier and persistence criterion, plus E4. It supports only persistent traces within that carrier, observable, and window, and may submit a predefined candidate historical variable to G3. Alone, it does not establish future paths, intertemporal reproduction, repair windows, or eligibility for action. An old record that still exists is only a clue to a trace, not a complete historical causal conclusion. [T-main；T-H5]","HV05-R4-carried-reproduction is another intertemporal explanation: G2 already covers the claimed subsequent outcome/window, and §4.5 is used to check current capability, rule, resource, or relational carriers; retention/replenishment over multiple cycles; actual continuity; and discriminating causal material. It does not require an additional G3 increment beyond the current state, but cannot extrapolate long-term results directly from a single functional effect. R2 and R4 are different explanatory routes and must not subsume each other. [T-main；T-G3]","Together the five routes provide precise ceilings: R0 classification; R1 bounded functional effects; R2 historical-increment intertemporal candidates; R3 persistent traces; R4 carrier-transmitted intertemporal candidates. The weaker language of persistence in R3 cannot secure R2/R4 conclusions. Conversely, lack of G3 support does not negate carrier transmission already supported by independent channel evidence. No route creates an unlimited obligation to bear burdens. [T-main]","Register CV and RS separately along the nine axes. Task cycles, maintenance backlogs, schedules, recovery lags, cost flows, alternative paths, and authority to stop each require material. The six types of responsibility concern conduct, decisions, authorization, supervision, benefits, and remedies; they cannot be collapsed into one ‘responsible person.’ If the task asks only whether one task is feasible, collection of every private burden is not mandatory. If overall burden reduction is claimed, management, standby work, substitutes' conditions, and continuity require proportionate examination. [T-main]","Those taking up work with limited permissions, informally, or without the ability to exit belong to an exclusion zone that cannot be ignored. They may challenge tasks, resources, costs, restrictions on stopping, and responsibility assignments, and obtain review outside the original allocation/attribution chain. Authorized rollback must revoke erroneous task-resource or attribution states, actually restore records and task status, and implement authorized remedies. Shifting the burden to another person without a voice cannot be called completed correction. [T-main]","The 2005 three-state Cash and Counseling report separates actual personal assistance, spending plans, counseling, employer tasks, and fiscal tasks. It provides actual program-design material for separating uptake labor from coordination and management, but two pages of the report cannot prove that total burdens vanished or all support arrived. Here it serves only as an R0-style reference for division of work, without claiming formal G2/G3/H5 support. [P-CC]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV05. Interpretation: This is a short identifier within the card collection, used to track the same interface; it does not establish a real-world object. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV05. Interpretation: The qualified namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidentiary status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Action-uptake layer. Interpretation: The name offers a reading entry point, not an automatic label for specific people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: The uptake carrier CV for execution, transmission, maintenance, recording, care, and repair must be registered separately from the responsible party RS, cost bearers, beneficiaries, and authority to stop. Interpretation: This proposition must be read together with the conditional routes and ceilings below; an unconditional causal or normative conclusion cannot be extracted from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Human structures requiring sustained action, maintenance, care, or execution. Interpretation: Scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean every object within that scope has empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: An executor with limited permissions is assumed to be the principal responsible party, or capacity for uptake is treated as an obligation. Interpretation: Pausing targets the dependent judgment or adoption. Independently established descriptions may remain, but invalid premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: Inferential dependencies provide the upstream content needed to establish a proposition. Upstream candidate/supported status must be carried through unchanged; citation does not automatically upgrade it. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: 1. H2. Interpretation: This card specializes the operational classification rule H2. It helps distinguish CV from RS; correct classification does not confer empirical causal truth. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV05-R0-carrier-responsibility-split；；claim_level=descriptive_classification；；when=CV, cost bearers of the same type, beneficiaries, authority to stop, RS, resources, and capacity can be registered separately under H2.；；additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set)；；additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set)；；allowed_conclusion=Register current uptake carriers, tasks, costs, capacity, authority to stop, responsibility types, and uptake gaps.；；result_ceiling=Current classification and gap description only; capacity for uptake does not become an obligation, and CV does not become RS.；2. route_id=HV05-R1-functional-carrier-effect；；claim_level=conditional_effect；；when=A qualifying G2-instance shows a channel effect exceeding the threshold for specified carrier replacement, interruption, replenishment, or load reduction on a preselected functional outcome.；；additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance；；additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL、E4；；allowed_conclusion=Register candidate uptake effects of the specified carrier on measured dimensions of function, capacity, delay, or loss.；；result_ceiling=Unmeasured dimensions remain unknown; functional effects must not directly generate responsibility, obligations of sacrifice, or authorization to reallocate resources.；3. route_id=HV05-R2-intertemporal-reproduction；；claim_level=intertemporal_explanation；；when=The current uptake channel has G2-instance support, and a G3-instance shows that its historical variable provides a conditional increment for subsequent uptake or reproduction outcomes.；；additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance、G3-instance；；additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL、E4；；allowed_conclusion=Register historical-increment candidates for intertemporal uptake or reproduction beyond the current state, within the specified window and carrier.；；result_ceiling=No inference of historical fate, irreversibility, responsibility assignment, or an obligation to continue bearing burdens.；4. route_id=HV05-R3-historical-carrier-trace；；claim_level=descriptive_classification；；when=An H5-instance achieves supported status for the uniquely preselected concrete carrier and persistence criterion.；；additional_inferential_requires=H5-instance；；additional_protocol_requires=E4；；allowed_conclusion=Register persistent human traces within the specified carrier, trace observable, and window, and submit a predefined candidate historical variable to a G3-instance.；；result_ceiling=An H5-instance does not establish future path effects, intertemporal reproduction, repair windows, responsibility, or action; these conclusions require evidence under the respective carrier or historical-increment routes, outcome contracts, and normative procedures.；5. route_id=HV05-R4-carried-reproduction；；claim_level=intertemporal_explanation；；when=A qualifying G2-instance supports the specified uptake channel's effect within the claimed subsequent outcome and window, and §4.5 is used to verify current capability, rule, resource, or relational carriers, retention or replenishment over multiple cycles, actual continuity, and discriminating causal material.；；additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance；；additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL、E4；；allowed_conclusion=Register carrier-transmitted candidates for intertemporal uptake or reproduction within the specified carrier, cycles, and outcome.；；result_ceiling=Do not register a historical increment beyond the current state, extrapolate long-term results from a single functional effect, or derive an obligation or authorization to continue bearing burdens from uptake facts. Interpretation: The five routes are individually independent: R0 current classification; R1 G2 bounded function; R2 G2 + G3 historical increment; R3 H5 traces for a unique carrier/persistence criterion; R4 G2 subsequent window + continuity of current carriers across cycles. Conditions and conclusions must not be interchanged. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. Uptake gaps; 2. Task-resource mismatches; 3. Separation of responsibilities. Interpretation: These are permitted directions for conclusions; which one is actually reached, and at what strength, remains bounded by the selected route and material. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. The most visible person is the principal responsible party; 2. Ability to bear a burden means an obligation to bear it; 3. Responsibility is assigned solely because a facility or procedure performs a function; organizational responsibility requires separate agent eligibility and specific rules. Interpretation: These are common overreaches that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; examine the actual inference and subsequent use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: individual task or uptake event, carrier case, uptake population, cost and capacity distributions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: worksite, team or organizational boundaries, digital systems, and cross-domain service scope; T=time span: task cycles, maintenance windows, recovery lags, and periods of responsibility; O=organizational level: executing roles, teams, organizations, institutions, through governance ecosystems; C=causal level: execution events, task–resource interaction mechanisms, meso-level uptake structures, institutional responsibility arrangements, and system conditions; R=observational resolution: original task logs, uptake sequences, carrier cases, cost and capacity distributions, performance indicators, and summaries, with compression losses registered; I=impact scope: direct uptake providers, service dependents, indirect beneficiaries or cost-bearing positions, second-order spillovers, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network-topological scope: uptake dependencies, alternative paths, single-point bottlenecks, and cross-domain service networks; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for task assignment, stopping, resource adjustment, attribution of responsibility, and remedies; register the scales of CV and RS separately. Interpretation: Register all nine axes individually. In particular, impact scope I does not generate jurisdiction J, and compression losses must remain recorded when observational resolution R decreases. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: People, positions, procedures, facilities, or institutions that actually execute, transmit, maintain, record, provide care, or repair. Interpretation: This describes the object of the interface. When selecting a low-threshold descriptive route, functional meanings in names must not be treated as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. CV is not RS; 2. Register costs and benefits separately; 3. The right to stop. Interpretation: Invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values remain unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions for elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Task aggregation; 2. Representation and delegation; 3. Six kinds of responsibility; 4. External costs. Interpretation: Elevation is not copying the original table at a larger size. When added material about populations, interfaces, and responsibility positions is incomplete, limit conclusions depending on it. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Uptake carriers and responsible parties may change across levels. Interpretation: The same term may change meaning when the object or scale changes. Reestablish the correspondence rather than assuming the old meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Nonhuman processes without requirements for subject action, responsibility, or maintenance. Interpretation: Inapplicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of interfaces for human norms, responsibility, and experience; they do not deny that other theories may study those objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. Directly equating individual uptake with organizational responsibility. Interpretation: Inferences from a part to the whole need separate evidence. Broad influence, grand names, or formal resemblance cannot supply representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: 1. Sufficient; 2. Fragile; 3. Overloaded; 4. Broken; 5. Replaced. Interpretation: States are descriptive categories requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people. State changes need windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Logs of actual task execution, maintenance, recording, and repair; 2. Flows of resources, capacity, time, and costs; 3. Records of stopping rights, replacement arrangements, and uptake transfers; 4. Grounds concerning decisions, authorization, oversight, benefit, and remedies. Interpretation: Observable clues indicate where to seek material. One clue’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. Task flows; 2. Working time and resources; 3. Maintenance records; 4. Stopping and refusal records; 5. If the target concerns total burden, separately examine management, standby, replacement-provider conditions, and continuity; individual-task judgments do not require collecting all of these. Interpretation: Evidence for an individual task need not expand without limit. Claims about total burden require the additional checks on management, standby, replacement conditions, and continuity. Evidence strength must be proportionate to the endpoint asked about. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface contents (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Generative needs 2. Resources 3. Authorization 4. Roles. Interpretation: Resources, roles, authorization, and generative needs are inputs to examine. “Someone can do it” must not be treated as their consent or obligation. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card: 1. Realization of state transitions 2. Cost distributions 3. Structural load. Interpretation: Variable flows indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this interface. Passing a reference does not guarantee a downstream conclusion, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register scheduling, maintenance cycles, backlogs, and recovery lags. Interpretation: Occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may happen at different times. Short-term material cannot automatically certify what lies beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record hidden labor, informal care, and costs beyond the boundary. Interpretation: Tie uncertainty to specific material and positions; do not write only an overall confidence score that hides positions never covered. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Low-authority, informal, and non-exitable uptake providers. Interpretation: An exclusion zone limits the coverage of current inference; it does not delete these people or facts from the world. Unavailability for safety reasons is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Uptake providers; 2. Beneficiaries; 3. Service recipients; 4. Replacements. Interpretation: Register both directly and indirectly affected people. Visible members do not represent everyone bearing costs. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Personnel; 2. Positions; 3. Procedures; 4. Facilities; 5. Institutions. Interpretation: A carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something. Its name, completion of construction, and delivery of the required function are different evidentiary matters. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Actors responsible for conduct; 2. Decisions; 3. Authorization; 4. Oversight; 5. Benefit; 6. Remedies. Interpretation: Determine the six kinds of responsibility separately from actual rules, facts, and subject eligibility. One person may hold several kinds, but low-authority execution alone cannot establish all responsibility. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: The fact of uptake does not generate an obligation to continue bearing it. Interpretation: Distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. An established fact does not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: R0 is limited to typing uptake and responsibility; R1 to the identified carrier-function effect; R3 to persistent traces supported by H5; R4 explains carrier-based reproduction under §4.5 and channel evidence covering subsequent windows; R2 explains historical increments beyond the current representation only with additional G3 support. Material and windows impose their respective limits; no route replaces the others, and none generates an unlimited duty to bear burdens. Interpretation: Guard especially against three substitutions: H5 traces are not G3 historical increments; a one-time G2 function is not cross-period reproduction; and unsupported G3 does not automatically negate independently evidenced R4 carrier transmission. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable generates only descriptions of CV, RS, costs, capacity, stopping rights, and uptake gaps, along with needs for load reduction, added resources, or redistribution. It does not authorize task adjustment, resource allocation, attribution of responsibility, or protection. Every real adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: The interface itself generates no power to act in reality. Actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protection, and duration. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. The most visible low-authority executor is not the party responsible for decisions, authorization, or benefit; 2. An automated facility carries out transmission tasks but cannot bear moral or legal responsibility. Interpretation: Use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrower scope or renewed evidence collection, not automatic rejection of every limited use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, uptake providers may challenge tasks, resources, costs, restrictions on stopping rights, and attributions of responsibility through safe, accessible channels protected against retaliation, triggering review independent of the original task-assignment or responsibility-attribution chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and able to trigger independent review. A name alone does not establish authority to change errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, designated responsible persons must revoke erroneous task, resource, or responsibility-attribution states within specified time limits, actually restore previous task and record states, and execute authorized remedies, preserving versions and completion verification. Interpretation: Within valid authority, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion. Editing document text does not mean rights and resources have returned to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["First use H2 to separate CV, RS, cost bearers, beneficiaries, and stopping rights.","R0 describes current resources, capacity, and gaps, preserving hidden labor.","R1 uses G2 to examine the specified functional dimension, without turning local improvement into total burden reduction.","R3 uses H5 to examine traces on a uniquely identified carrier; future effects require another route.","Cross-period R2 requires G3 historical increments beyond the current representation; R4 is established through current-carrier continuity across cycles and subsequent channel evidence.","Check responsibility, norms, and authority separately, protecting uptake providers’ appeals and right to stop.","After correction is authorized, actually revoke erroneous tasks and attributions of responsibility, and verify that remedies arrive."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"CV uptake carrier","leftText":"Actually executes or maintains a function.","rightTitle":"RS responsible party","rightText":"Specific kinds of responsibility and eligibility require separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"R3 persistent traces","leftText":"H5 supports preservation on a carrier.","rightTitle":"R2 historical increments","rightText":"G3 tests subsequent increments beyond the current representation."},{"leftTitle":"R4 carrier transmission","leftText":"The current carrier is retained and replenished across cycles, with actual continuity.","rightTitle":"A one-time functional effect","rightText":"Does not automatically establish long-term reproduction."},{"leftTitle":"Reduced execution work","leftText":"A replacement takes over a particular task.","rightTitle":"Reduced total burden","rightText":"Management, standby, expenses, and replacement conditions require separate checks."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the original card: the most visible low-authority executor is not the party responsible for decisions, authorization, or benefit.","Counterexample in the original card: an automated facility’s transmission tasks do not give it moral or legal responsibility.","Teaching counterexample: an old file preserved for ten years is used to claim proof of future reproduction and an irreversible path.","Boundary in the source: ability to bear a task does not mean an obligation to do so; functional effects do not confer authority to adjust tasks or resources."],"questions":["Is the busiest person necessarily chiefly responsible?","Can H5-supported traces establish future reproduction?","Without G3, does every explanation of uptake across periods fail?","If help reduces execution hours, can a local outcome be recognized?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/figures/hv05-roles.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Division of work in the three-state Cash and Counseling program","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Division of work in the three-state Cash and Counseling program","text":"The 2005 report on programs in Arkansas, New Jersey, and Florida separately describes personal assistance, spending plans, counseling, and fiscal and employer support.","sourceLabel":"Cash and Counseling：Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","sourceUrl":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","sourceLocator":"Report of 2005-05-16, printed pages 7–8 (PDF 19–20), spending plans and “Counseling and Fiscal Services”","boundary":"A designed division of work does not establish that all support actually arrived or that total burden disappeared. This is not a formal G/H instance and is not a real personal-care plan.","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.5","start_line":4011,"end_line":4076,"startLine":4011,"endLine":4076}],"outputId":"section-a-5","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one task decreases while total burden remains undetermined","text":"An artificial care task originally requires 4 hours of execution, 1 hour of management, and 2 hours of standby. A replacement takes over the 4 execution hours, while the original provider still manages and remains on standby. A reduction of 4 execution hours can be recognized. Under a simple common time measure, 3 hours remain for the original provider, not zero. Actual total burden also includes overlapping time, psychological and resource factors, and more; 7 and 3 cannot simply be treated as complete welfare quantities.","assumptions":"All hours are artificial and assumed not to overlap; they only illustrate the difference between task and total endpoints.","boundary":"The remaining 3 hours do not negate the reduction of 4, and continuity requirements must not impose unlimited remaining responsibility on the original provider."},{"title":"Teaching construction: three historical claims require separate evidence","text":"A fictional institution still preserves old training records: a trace candidate. Currently trained personnel apply their skills to service each period and receive replenishment: a carrier-continuity candidate. Under the same current representation of skills, rules, and resources, different historical paths still improve subsequent prediction or identification: a historical-increment candidate. These respectively require H5, the G2 and carrier-cycle material needed for R4, and the G2/G3 needed for R2. Preserved records alone cannot certify all three.","assumptions":"The institution, material, and comparisons are artificial; there are no formal instance results.","boundary":"“Candidate” is not supported. Increments beyond the current representation and transmission through current carriers neither exclude nor prove one another."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: the most visible low-authority executor is not the party responsible for decisions, authorization, or benefit.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: an automated facility’s transmission tasks do not give it moral or legal responsibility.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: an old file preserved for ten years is used to claim proof of future reproduction and an irreversible path.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: ability to bear a task does not mean an obligation to do so; functional effects do not confer authority to adjust tasks or resources.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is the busiest person necessarily chiefly responsible?","answer":"No. CV, costs, and the six kinds of RS require separate checks; visible execution cannot establish responsibility for decisions or authorization.","basis":"H2 and the inferential leaps prohibited by this card."},{"question":"Can H5-supported traces establish future reproduction?","answer":"No. R3 establishes traces only; cross-period claims need separate evidence through R2 or R4.","basis":"R3 ceiling."},{"question":"Without G3, does every explanation of uptake across periods fail?","answer":"No. R4 explains carrier transmission through the current carrier, continuity over multiple cycles, and G2 evidence covering subsequent periods; it cannot pose as a G3 increment.","basis":"Difference between R4 and R2."},{"question":"If help reduces execution hours, can a local outcome be recognized?","answer":"Yes. Recognize it for the specified task and conditions; total-burden questions require additional checks on management, standby, and replacement-provider conditions.","basis":"Item 5 of evidence."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/figures/hv05-roles.png","alt":"Register uptake carriers, cost bearers, beneficiaries, stopping rights, and the six kinds of responsibility separately. Ability does not imply obligation.","caption":"Based on A.5 H2 typing."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/figures/hv05-routes.png","alt":"R0 typing; R1 G2 function; R2 G2 plus G3 increments; R3 H5 traces; R4 G2 and carrier continuity. They are not interchangeable.","caption":"Based on the five complete routes in A.5 and §4.5."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/figures/hv05-burden.png","alt":"Examine execution, management, standby, expenses, and replacement conditions separately. Individual-task outcomes may hold; overall claims require overall material.","caption":"Based on item 5 of A.5 evidence; see the text for the artificial 4/1/2-hour example."}],"diagramBrief":"The three diagrams separately distinguish uptake from responsibility, evidence for the five routes, and local from total burdens. Route numbers are not stages and cannot be upgraded sequentially; the diagrams only indicate each route’s evidence requirements.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["This topic contains no qualified G2/G3/H5 instance records; explanation of the five routes confers no supported status.","Actual burden reduction, persistent continuity, subject responsibility, and action authority require independent checks according to the chosen outcome and window."],"taskId":"S-A-5","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.5, Chinese historical source lines 3832–3897","claim_support":["All 39 fields of HV05 and its five routes R0–R4; task and total-burden endpoints."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-H2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.1, Chinese source lines 1469–1485","claim_support":["H2 operational typing does not carry empirical truth automatically."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["Effects of a specified carrier channel."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G3","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.5, Chinese source lines 620–641","claim_support":["Different routes for historical increments and transmission through current carriers."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-H5","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.2, Chinese source lines 586–601","claim_support":["H5’s uniquely identified carrier, outcome family, and persistence criteria."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CC","title":"Cash and Counseling：Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","url":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005-05-16","locator":"Report of 2005-05-16, printed pages 7–8 (PDF 19–20), spending plans and “Counseling and Fiscal Services”","claim_support":["The three-state program separately lists personal assistance, spending plans, employer assistance, counseling, and fiscal-management support."],"limits":["The original guide’s author read the complete pages 19–20 of the previously obtained original PDF. Online retrieval during that check returned 403, so the saved original was used for verification. Only the historical design’s division of work is used; it does not establish full support delivery, disappearance of coordination burdens, or formal establishment of any G/H instance, and offers no current care or legal advice."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.5 HV05 Action Uptake Layer (Complete Interface Card); Chinese source lines 3918–3983; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/source/S-A-5.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"dbf33e1ab0517db0b5f5fc417b78dbef83d3efe7abd748729b2a6a50bfc5e814","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-05.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-05.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-05.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,f){const t=r("SectionLesson");return a(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV05 Action Uptake Layer (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.5 HV05 Action Uptake Layer (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-5}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV05</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV05</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Action Uptake Layer</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>Uptake carriers CV that execute, transmit, maintain, record, provide care, or repair must be recorded separately from responsible parties RS, cost bearers, beneficiaries, and rights to stop.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human structures requiring sustained action, maintenance, care, or execution</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>People who execute with limited authority are classified by default as primarily responsible, or the capability to take up work is treated as an obligation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. EVIDENCE<br>2. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>1. H2</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV05-R0-carrier-responsibility-split;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=CV, bearers of costs of the same type, beneficiaries, rights to stop, RS, resources, and capacity can be recorded separately under H2;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Register current uptake carriers, tasks, costs, capacity, rights to stop, types of responsibility, and gaps in uptake;<br>result_ceiling=Only the current classification and description of gaps. The capability to take up work does not become an obligation, and CV does not become RS.<br>2. route_id=HV05-R1-functional-carrier-effect;<br>claim_level=conditional_effect;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance shows that replacing, interrupting, replenishing, or reducing load on the specified carrier has a channel effect above the threshold on the preselected functional outcome;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register the candidate uptake effect of the specified carrier on the measured dimensions of function, capacity, delay, or loss;<br>result_ceiling=Unmeasured dimensions remain unknown. Functional effects must not directly generate responsibility, a duty to make sacrifices, or authorization to reallocate resources.<br>3. route_id=HV05-R2-intertemporal-reproduction;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=The current uptake channel already has support from a G2-instance, and a G3-instance shows that its historical variables provide a conditional increment for subsequent uptake or reproduction outcomes;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, G3-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for intertemporal uptake or reproduction with a historical increment beyond the current state, within the specified window and carrier;<br>result_ceiling=Do not infer historical inevitability, irreversibility, attribution of responsibility, or a duty to continue taking up the work.<br>4. route_id=HV05-R3-historical-carrier-trace;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=An H5-instance returns supported for the specific carrier and durability criterion, with exactly one of each selected in advance;<br>additional_inferential_requires=H5-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register durable traces in the human domain within the specified carrier, trace observables, and window, and submit predefined candidate historical variables to a G3-instance;<br>result_ceiling=An H5-instance does not establish future path effects, intertemporal reproduction, a repair window, responsibility, or action. Obtain evidence for these conclusions separately under the carrier or historical-increment routes, outcome contracts, and normative procedures.<br>5. route_id=HV05-R4-carried-reproduction;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance supports the effect of the specified uptake channel on the declared subsequent outcomes within the stated window, and the current carriers of capabilities, rules, resources, or relationships, retention or replenishment across several cycles, actual continuity, and discriminating causal materials are verified under §4.5;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for intertemporal uptake or reproduction through carrier transmission within the specified carriers, cycles, and outcomes;<br>result_ceiling=Do not register a historical increment beyond the current state or extrapolate long-term effects from a single functional effect. The fact of uptake does not generate a duty or authorization to continue taking up the work.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Gaps in uptake. 2. Mismatches between tasks and resources. 3. Distinctions among responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Treating the most visible person as primarily responsible<br>2. Inferring that someone ought to take up work because they can<br>3. Assigning responsibility solely because facilities or procedures execute a function. Organizational responsibility is assessed separately under the organization’s standing as a subject and the specific rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual tasks or uptake events, cases involving carriers, the uptake population, distributions of costs and capacity, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers work sites, team or organizational boundaries, digital systems, and the reach of services across domains. T=Time span covers task cycles, maintenance windows, recovery lags, and the period in which responsibility applies. O=Organizational level covers execution roles, teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers events of execution, mechanisms of interaction between tasks and resources, meso-level uptake structures, institutional arrangements of responsibility, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original task logs, sequences of uptake, cases involving carriers, distributions of costs and capacity, performance metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers those directly taking up the work, people dependent on services, positions that indirectly receive benefits or bear costs, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers dependencies in uptake, alternative paths, single-point bottlenecks, and service networks across domains. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for task assignment, stopping, resource adjustments, attribution of responsibility, and remedy. Register scales separately for CV and RS</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>People, positions, procedures, facilities, or institutions that actually execute, transmit, maintain, record, provide care, or repair</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. CV does not amount to RS<br>2. Costs and benefits are recorded separately<br>3. Rights to stop</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Aggregation of tasks<br>2. Representation and delegation<br>3. Six kinds of responsibility<br>4. External costs</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Uptake carriers and responsible parties can change with the level</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Nonhuman processes without action by a subject, responsibility, or maintenance requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Equating uptake by an individual directly with organizational responsibility</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Adequate<br>2. Fragile<br>3. Overloaded<br>4. Disrupted<br>5. Replaced</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Logs of actual task execution, maintenance, recording, and repair<br>2. Flows of resources, capacity, time, and costs<br>3. Records of rights to stop, alternative arrangements, and transfer of uptake<br>4. Grounds for decisions, authorization, oversight, benefit, and remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Task flows<br>2. Working hours and resources<br>3. Maintenance records<br>4. Records of stopping and refusal<br>5. If the objective concerns overall burden, check management, being on call, conditions for those taking over, and continuity separately. A judgment about one task need not collect all of these</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Demand arising from generation. 2. Resources. 3. Authorization. 4. Roles</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Realization of state transitions. 2. Cost distribution. 3. Structural load</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Register schedules, maintenance cycles, backlogs, and recovery lags</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record hidden labor, informal care, and costs outside the boundary</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>People taking up work with limited authority, informally, or without the ability to exit</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. People taking up the work<br>2. Beneficiaries<br>3. People served<br>4. Replacements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Personnel<br>2. Positions<br>3. Procedures<br>4. Facilities<br>5. Institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Parties responsible for conduct<br>2. Parties responsible for decisions<br>3. Parties responsible for authorization<br>4. Parties responsible for oversight<br>5. Parties responsible as beneficiaries<br>6. Parties responsible for remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>The fact of uptake does not generate a duty to continue taking up the work</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>R0 is limited to classification of uptake and responsibility. R1 is limited to identified functional effects of carriers. R3 is limited to durable traces supported by H5. R4 explains reproduction through carriers under §4.5 and with channel evidence covering subsequent windows. R2 explains historical increments beyond the current representation only with additional G3. Each route is limited by its own materials and window. No route may substitute for the others or create an unlimited duty to take up the work.</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of CV, RS, costs, capacity, rights to stop, and gaps in uptake, along with needs to reduce load, add resources, or reallocate them. It does not authorize changes in tasks, resource allocation, attribution of responsibility, or protective action. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. The most visible person with limited execution authority is not the party responsible for decisions or authorization, or responsible as a beneficiary<br>2. An automated facility takes up transmission tasks but cannot bear moral or legal responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, people taking up work can challenge tasks, resources, costs, restrictions on the right to stop, and attribution of responsibility through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original chain of task assignment or attribution</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke erroneous task, resource, or responsibility statuses, actually restore previous task and record states, and carry out authorized remedies. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.5"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(d,[["render",l]]);export{g as __pageData,y as default};
